1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,840
At the end of every year, we buy the gifts, deck the halls, and trim the tree.

2
00:00:06,980 --> 00:00:15,500
Thousands of lights, miles of wire, latex, motors, hardware, software, and fruitcake.

3
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:25,180
Ho, ho, hold on tight as we unwrap the year-round planning, planting, and packing that makes it all come together.

4
00:00:25,181 --> 00:00:30,080
Now, Christmas Tech on Modern Marvels

5
00:00:45,700 --> 00:00:49,500
Christmas is observed by nearly one-third of the world's population

6
00:00:49,501 --> 00:00:53,960
and probably more if you count the non-Christians

7
00:00:53,961 --> 00:00:56,260
who incorporate some of the more secular traditions

8
00:00:56,261 --> 00:00:57,440
into their winter season.

9
00:01:00,320 --> 00:01:02,780
So as holy days and holidays go,

10
00:01:03,480 --> 00:01:04,780
Christmas is a pretty big deal.

11
00:01:06,880 --> 00:01:08,360
Holiday makers go all out.

12
00:01:09,960 --> 00:01:11,240
Trees heavy with decorations,

13
00:01:12,080 --> 00:01:13,320
elaborate lighting schemes,

14
00:01:14,420 --> 00:01:15,820
edibles and presents galore.

15
00:01:15,821 --> 00:01:21,120
In a city as culturally diverse as New York City,

16
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:27,580
many regional traditions come together to form one spectacular display of Christmas spirit.

17
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:34,060
Each year, the Big Apple tries to outdo the last,

18
00:01:34,580 --> 00:01:38,480
with millions of colored lights, illuminating buildings and streets,

19
00:01:38,481 --> 00:01:45,360
and impressive store window decorations encouraging revelers to buy, buy, buy.

20
00:01:45,820 --> 00:01:51,540
Four, three, two, four.

21
00:01:53,540 --> 00:01:54,900
Hey, Vincent!

22
00:01:56,320 --> 00:02:01,800
The focal point of it all, the Rockefeller Center Tree,

23
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:05,140
presides majestically over a seasonal skating rink

24
00:02:05,141 --> 00:02:08,680
and attracts throngs of visitors to midtown Manhattan.

25
00:02:10,820 --> 00:02:14,520
It usually stands between 75 and 90 feet tall.

26
00:02:14,521 --> 00:02:17,340
and is at least 35 feet wide.

27
00:02:19,380 --> 00:02:22,860
Each year, the giant conifer is chosen many months in advance.

28
00:02:24,840 --> 00:02:28,180
In the middle of the winter, when the citrus trees have no leaves,

29
00:02:28,720 --> 00:02:32,220
I look by helicopter for a giant Norway spruce.

30
00:02:35,460 --> 00:02:39,440
It's a non-native tree that's found in people's front or backyards,

31
00:02:39,840 --> 00:02:44,340
and I look on my computer and mark the GPS location of the tree.

32
00:02:44,520 --> 00:02:48,320
and I'm very happy when I find one and so are the people.

33
00:02:48,440 --> 00:02:51,200
It's a great surprise when I knock on their door and say,

34
00:02:51,280 --> 00:02:53,840
I'm from Rockefeller Center, I'm interested in your tree.

35
00:02:56,100 --> 00:02:59,100
In early November, the selected tree is prepped for cutting.

36
00:03:00,240 --> 00:03:04,640
We use a 160-ton crane to corset the tree so it can fold up.

37
00:03:04,641 --> 00:03:18,641
A crane is positioned over the tree and slowly draws up the branches, ties it up more tightly

38
00:03:18,720 --> 00:03:22,060
so the tree can fit on a specially designed truck.

39
00:03:23,120 --> 00:03:26,440
The tree has a truck that's an accordion.

40
00:03:26,560 --> 00:03:32,160
It can stretch or contract depending on the tree, whether it's 80 feet or 90 feet or 100 feet.

41
00:03:32,161 --> 00:03:36,160
The chosen tree is hailed from many different areas.

42
00:03:36,161 --> 00:03:40,160
It's transported on the custom-made telescoping trailer

43
00:03:40,161 --> 00:03:44,160
that travels a carefully planned route with a police escort.

44
00:03:44,161 --> 00:03:50,160
Depending on the distance, sometimes the truck hitches a ride on a plane.

45
00:03:50,161 --> 00:03:52,160
Or barge.

46
00:03:56,160 --> 00:04:00,160
Once the tree arrives at Rockefeller Center, workers raise and stabilize it.

47
00:04:02,840 --> 00:04:05,420
The tree is hoisted into place and held by a crane,

48
00:04:05,600 --> 00:04:08,720
and four cables are attached that go off two to the building

49
00:04:08,721 --> 00:04:11,740
and two to the wall of the ice rink from the midpoint of the tree.

50
00:04:12,260 --> 00:04:16,320
And the tree has a stainless steel spike driven into the bottom of the trunk,

51
00:04:16,840 --> 00:04:20,200
and that's placed into a hole or a cup and then holds it as a pinpoint

52
00:04:20,201 --> 00:04:22,000
so that the bottom can't move.

53
00:04:22,620 --> 00:04:26,840
We have master riggers and engineers who calculate the weight loads

54
00:04:26,841 --> 00:04:29,760
and the weight load if there's snow or ice or water on the tree.

55
00:04:29,761 --> 00:04:41,880
Balancing precariously on eight stories of scaffolding, two dozen electricians decorate the tree with 30,000 colored lights attached to five miles of wiring.

56
00:04:44,720 --> 00:04:51,940
A nine-and-a-half-foot diameter crystal star, crafted by Swarovski of Austria, is placed atop the tree.

57
00:04:51,941 --> 00:04:56,340
The Swarovski star weighs close to 600 pounds.

58
00:04:56,341 --> 00:05:00,660
25,000 crystals make one million facets,

59
00:05:00,661 --> 00:05:04,160
which ensure that the star sparkles day and night

60
00:05:04,161 --> 00:05:06,460
on top of the Rockefeller Center Christmas tree.

61
00:05:10,660 --> 00:05:12,860
A few days after every Thanksgiving,

62
00:05:13,340 --> 00:05:16,040
a huge celebrity-studded crowd of thousands gathers

63
00:05:16,041 --> 00:05:18,460
as the tree lights up for the first time.

64
00:05:18,900 --> 00:05:20,440
Good times for everyone,

65
00:05:20,441 --> 00:05:24,980
but the Rockefeller Center Christmas tree tradition was born in bad times.

66
00:05:25,120 --> 00:05:27,640
Rockefeller Center was built during the Great Depression.

67
00:05:28,220 --> 00:05:30,580
In 1931, the first year of construction,

68
00:05:31,060 --> 00:05:33,840
the workers felt fortunate to have a job and to have a paycheck

69
00:05:33,841 --> 00:05:37,500
and they got the first Christmas tree, a 20-foot balsam fir,

70
00:05:37,640 --> 00:05:39,160
to celebrate the holidays.

71
00:05:41,100 --> 00:05:42,960
The custom has never faltered,

72
00:05:43,480 --> 00:05:44,940
not even during the war years,

73
00:05:44,941 --> 00:05:47,540
nor in 2001 after 9-11.

74
00:05:50,440 --> 00:06:02,200
This popular contemporary tradition likely finds its roots in ancient times.

75
00:06:02,201 --> 00:06:06,620
Some scholars believe that the pagan cultures of northern Europe associated evergreens with

76
00:06:06,621 --> 00:06:12,580
the renewal of life, because they stayed green throughout winter.

77
00:06:12,581 --> 00:06:17,060
This symbol was eventually attached to the Roman Winter Feast, celebrated on the winter

78
00:06:17,061 --> 00:06:22,420
solstice day of December 25th, the day with the least sunlight, according to the Julian

79
00:06:22,421 --> 00:06:23,121
calendar.

80
00:06:27,160 --> 00:06:33,860
As Christianity prevailed, the festival became the celebration of Christ's birth, taking

81
00:06:33,861 --> 00:06:36,340
with it some of the pagan symbols to ease the transition.

82
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:44,200
In the Middle Ages, Germanic tribes brought the revered evergreens indoors as a sign of

83
00:06:44,201 --> 00:06:45,360
hope for the coming spring.

84
00:06:47,060 --> 00:06:51,100
As these cultures moved throughout Europe, their traditions followed.

85
00:06:51,720 --> 00:06:55,980
It's said that Prince Albert, who was married to Queen Victoria of England, of course,

86
00:06:56,340 --> 00:07:00,280
was instrumental in popularizing the Christmas tree in England.

87
00:07:00,281 --> 00:07:07,260
There were illustrations published in English newspapers of the royal family celebrating with a Christmas tree.

88
00:07:07,900 --> 00:07:15,360
So that really popularized the custom because British people of that time followed the fashions of the royal family and the nobility.

89
00:07:15,361 --> 00:07:22,720
You also have German people bringing the Christmas tree to America as they begin to emigrate in greater numbers in the 19th century.

90
00:07:24,680 --> 00:07:28,380
The tradition grew and spawned a flourishing trade.

91
00:07:30,200 --> 00:07:34,970
Of the roughly 30 million real trees sold in the U.S.

92
00:07:34,971 --> 00:07:42,980
each year, about 25% are harvested in Oregon, 11% in North Carolina, and 10% in Michigan.

93
00:07:43,940 --> 00:07:45,900
The rest come from all over the country.

94
00:07:48,060 --> 00:07:51,840
For those who love the fresh smell of a real evergreen in their living rooms,

95
00:07:52,460 --> 00:07:56,440
cut trees can be purchased and carted home from any of the lots that sprout up in November.

96
00:07:59,800 --> 00:08:02,560
Or maybe you prefer something a bit more highbrow.

97
00:08:05,740 --> 00:08:11,400
Yule Tree Farms of Aurora, Oregon, specially cultivates trees that will bear the tag of excellence.

98
00:08:12,980 --> 00:08:22,460
The 3,800-acre facility has introduced the first designer Christmas tree, turning a commodity

99
00:08:22,461 --> 00:08:26,380
into a trademarked brand name.

100
00:08:26,381 --> 00:08:34,200
Oregon's noble vintage fir is carefully bred to have the most desired characteristics.

101
00:08:34,201 --> 00:08:40,260
A bluish-green hue, soft upturned needles, and better needle retention than other types

102
00:08:40,261 --> 00:08:44,320
of evergreens.

103
00:08:44,321 --> 00:08:50,580
These premium trees retail for about 30% more than other trees, and represent less than 5%

104
00:08:50,581 --> 00:08:55,380
of the market.

105
00:08:55,381 --> 00:09:02,020
Yule tree farms also grow several other evergreen species.

106
00:09:02,021 --> 00:09:06,800
While just about every pine, fir and spruce has held the exalted position of family Christmas

107
00:09:06,801 --> 00:09:13,800
The most commonly used are the balsam, Fraser, and Douglas firs.

108
00:09:13,801 --> 00:09:16,800
This is a field of Douglas fir Christmas trees here.

109
00:09:16,801 --> 00:09:17,811
They're two years old.

110
00:09:17,812 --> 00:09:20,800
In this particular block, there's between three and four million.

111
00:09:20,801 --> 00:09:22,727
The average height here is 18 inches.

112
00:09:22,728 --> 00:09:25,800
They've reached their full growing potential for this year.

113
00:09:25,801 --> 00:09:31,800
After two growing seasons in the seedling nursery, the trees are transplanted into the fields.

114
00:09:31,801 --> 00:09:40,800
workers will continuously inspect the trees for disease sunburn fungus and unwanted pests

115
00:09:40,801 --> 00:09:45,060
but not all pests are unwanted

116
00:09:45,061 --> 00:09:51,800
while many other growers continue to randomly spray pesticides using helicopters

117
00:09:51,801 --> 00:09:56,640
yule tree farms employs a relatively new progressive pest control philosophy

118
00:09:56,641 --> 00:10:01,760
We use a program called Integrated Pest Management.

119
00:10:02,200 --> 00:10:06,540
There are predator bugs as well as the bugs that attack the trees.

120
00:10:06,740 --> 00:10:10,320
We support the predator insects that eat the other insects.

121
00:10:10,680 --> 00:10:13,840
We recognize that we need to be very careful with these pesticides.

122
00:10:14,260 --> 00:10:19,080
If we find a pocket where the population has increased to a point where it's going to cause damage,

123
00:10:19,180 --> 00:10:20,420
we try to hit that spot.

124
00:10:23,020 --> 00:10:26,580
Localizing pesticide spray over vast fields is a challenge.

125
00:10:27,580 --> 00:10:30,400
To do it by hand would be costly and painstaking.

126
00:10:32,360 --> 00:10:34,480
Heavy machinery could damage the ground.

127
00:10:35,760 --> 00:10:39,700
So it takes a special vehicle to tread lightly and spray precisely.

128
00:10:41,020 --> 00:10:45,640
The GK tracker allows the boom to be right on the trees

129
00:10:45,641 --> 00:10:48,060
and direct the spray exactly where it's needed

130
00:10:48,061 --> 00:10:51,360
and not have the risk of spray drift, which is a big concern.

131
00:10:51,361 --> 00:10:58,440
In the past, there wasn't a machine that could get across the wet ground here in Oregon.

132
00:10:58,441 --> 00:11:03,460
The sprayer that we use has a minimal impact.

133
00:11:03,461 --> 00:11:08,400
The footprint is less than what your heel is as you walk across the field.

134
00:11:08,401 --> 00:11:11,920
Part of that's due to the length of the track, but it's the disbursement of the weight of

135
00:11:11,921 --> 00:11:15,640
the machine.

136
00:11:15,641 --> 00:11:25,200
The trees grow in the fields for about eight to ten years to reach maturity.

137
00:11:25,201 --> 00:11:29,860
Once every growing season, a shearing team moves through the field, expertly swinging

138
00:11:29,861 --> 00:11:30,860
their blades.

139
00:11:30,861 --> 00:11:36,760
For shearing Christmas trees, you need three main pieces of equipment, a machete, clippers,

140
00:11:36,761 --> 00:11:37,760
and a leg protector.

141
00:11:37,761 --> 00:11:42,640
On this particular tree, we have the top splinted with a bamboo, and that's just to provide

142
00:11:42,641 --> 00:11:47,640
a nice straight leader for the tree, for a star or angel or anything else that would

143
00:11:47,641 --> 00:11:48,902
go on top of a tree.

144
00:11:48,903 --> 00:11:53,320
The next thing you watch for is the top whorl, the branches, the first

145
00:11:53,321 --> 00:11:55,294
whorl of branches that come out the tree.

146
00:11:55,295 --> 00:11:57,460
You want them to be about 8 to 10 inches long

147
00:11:57,461 --> 00:12:07,120
in length, and we just follow that line from the top all the way down to provide a nice

148
00:12:07,121 --> 00:12:12,740
smooth line that keeps the trees nice and full, yet providing access points for Christmas

149
00:12:12,741 --> 00:12:13,441
tree ornaments.

150
00:12:13,442 --> 00:12:17,040
When you're shearing a Christmas tree, you have to assess each tree as an individual.

151
00:12:17,460 --> 00:12:20,300
It's kind of like a haircut. Not everybody looks good with the same haircut.

152
00:12:24,820 --> 00:12:28,780
Just before the harvest, taggers mark the best trees according to height.

153
00:12:28,781 --> 00:12:34,720
Then it's time to cut him down

154
00:12:34,721 --> 00:12:38,740
Using a chainsaw with a safer and faster bow bar

155
00:12:38,741 --> 00:12:47,020
And next comes a step that's a huge relief for anyone

156
00:12:47,021 --> 00:12:49,420
Who's ever had to clean up the mess come January

157
00:12:54,460 --> 00:12:57,820
Mechanical shakers knock the excess needles off the tree

158
00:12:57,821 --> 00:13:05,040
Finally, the trees are sent through the baler to be trussed and twined

159
00:13:05,041 --> 00:13:11,660
And then loaded onto refrigerated trucks for delivery across North America

160
00:13:11,661 --> 00:13:14,080
To a tree lot near you

161
00:13:14,081 --> 00:13:23,300
The tree is just step one of many in the hull-decking extravaganza

162
00:13:23,301 --> 00:13:30,680
After all, what's a tree without strings of flashing, bubbling, flickering light strewn across it?

163
00:13:33,180 --> 00:13:38,620
In 2005, of the approximately 100 million households in the United States,

164
00:13:39,280 --> 00:13:41,520
28% had real Christmas trees,

165
00:13:42,180 --> 00:13:44,440
35% had artificial trees,

166
00:13:44,980 --> 00:13:48,120
and 37% had no trees.

167
00:13:49,460 --> 00:13:52,120
Christmas Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

168
00:13:52,121 --> 00:13:55,640
Turn to Christmas Tech on Modern Marvels.

169
00:13:57,560 --> 00:13:58,840
Tis the season.

170
00:13:59,740 --> 00:14:01,340
The season of lights.

171
00:14:07,140 --> 00:14:11,340
And it's pretty obvious at Mike Simkowski's house in Sherman Oaks, California.

172
00:14:13,480 --> 00:14:15,560
Here in the land of endless summer,

173
00:14:16,420 --> 00:14:21,420
Mike replicates the winter spirit by producing a captivating choreographed outdoor light show.

174
00:14:22,120 --> 00:14:24,780
to the delight or dismay of his neighbors.

175
00:14:28,520 --> 00:14:33,640
First, he programs selected music into a software program called Animation Director

176
00:14:33,641 --> 00:14:36,780
that will synchronize lighting cues to the music track.

177
00:14:39,940 --> 00:14:44,440
Then he rigs up props and lights that plug into controllers hidden around the yard.

178
00:14:44,441 --> 00:14:52,180
and the magic of technology takes over.

179
00:14:52,640 --> 00:14:54,300
The controllers are fed by the cables,

180
00:14:54,440 --> 00:14:56,600
which are fed from the controller called the monster brain,

181
00:14:56,760 --> 00:14:58,400
which sends the signals out to each controller

182
00:14:58,401 --> 00:15:02,080
and then tells the controller if it needs to be on or off,

183
00:15:02,240 --> 00:15:03,580
fade or dim or flicker.

184
00:15:03,940 --> 00:15:05,940
The highlight of the display is a 16-foot tree,

185
00:15:06,040 --> 00:15:07,060
which I created out of PVC.

186
00:15:07,840 --> 00:15:09,260
Inside the tree, I actually have a spotlight,

187
00:15:09,261 --> 00:15:11,320
which is a DMX-controlled intelligent light.

188
00:15:11,321 --> 00:15:16,640
The light is programmed to sweep the house and also project patterns onto the house,

189
00:15:16,641 --> 00:15:19,860
like falling snowflakes, sparkles, or whatever I can think of.

190
00:15:20,020 --> 00:15:22,520
The thing about the computer-controlled lighting is it's very efficient.

191
00:15:22,960 --> 00:15:27,100
So most lights are on and off quite a bit, so I'm never really drawing that much power at one time.

192
00:15:27,240 --> 00:15:30,180
So I don't see that much change in my electric bill each season.

193
00:15:32,020 --> 00:15:37,260
Mike and other outdoor decorating enthusiasts are taking Christmas into the 21st century.

194
00:15:41,320 --> 00:15:47,700
Step inside Mike's holiday home, where nothing says Merry Christmas like a tree with all the trimmings.

195
00:15:51,700 --> 00:15:54,920
Electric Christmas lights became popular in the 20th century.

196
00:15:56,080 --> 00:15:59,560
But the idea of illuminating a Christmas tree is centuries old.

197
00:16:03,900 --> 00:16:11,020
According to folklore, in the early 1500s, Martin Luther was the first to place candles on an evergreen in winter.

198
00:16:11,320 --> 00:16:16,220
to imitate twinkling stars, and to symbolize Christ as the light of the world.

199
00:16:19,520 --> 00:16:24,440
Still, there's no official documentation of a lit tree until well over a century later.

200
00:16:26,840 --> 00:16:32,340
Candles were put on trees because people were using candles anyway to celebrate Christmas.

201
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:36,620
For one, it's pretty dark in northern Europe at Christmastime,

202
00:16:36,680 --> 00:16:39,760
and they represented the birth of light after the winter solstice.

203
00:16:41,320 --> 00:16:45,860
But there were some obvious potential hazards associated with his practice.

204
00:16:45,940 --> 00:16:48,540
There are actually two things that are dangerous about it.

205
00:16:48,920 --> 00:16:53,500
One of them was, of course, having the candle fall over on a fairly dry tree.

206
00:16:54,380 --> 00:17:00,300
And that's why people would have a bucket of water on the floor whenever they lit up the Christmas tree.

207
00:17:00,840 --> 00:17:06,420
The other thing that nobody thinks about is the fact that little girls in particular and women

208
00:17:06,421 --> 00:17:08,640
would lean into the tree to look at something

209
00:17:08,641 --> 00:17:11,820
and set their clothes on fire from the lower candles.

210
00:17:15,040 --> 00:17:18,440
It was actually only around the late 19th century

211
00:17:18,441 --> 00:17:22,220
when people began to devise fairly secure and safe ways

212
00:17:22,221 --> 00:17:24,320
of attaching the candles to the trees.

213
00:17:24,620 --> 00:17:27,260
Of course, by that time, electric lights were invented

214
00:17:27,261 --> 00:17:31,560
and they would soon take over and make Christmas tree lighting a lot safer.

215
00:17:33,640 --> 00:17:35,560
At the turn of the 20th century,

216
00:17:35,561 --> 00:17:38,000
electricity was still in its infancy.

217
00:17:38,940 --> 00:17:41,440
Only the wealthy could afford the luxury of holiday lights.

218
00:17:42,580 --> 00:17:45,900
These were expensive and they were complicated to connect.

219
00:17:46,560 --> 00:17:49,220
So you had to hire an electrician to get them going.

220
00:17:50,480 --> 00:17:55,720
In 1903, General Electric introduced prefabbed series-wired light strings.

221
00:17:56,820 --> 00:17:59,680
The price tag for a string of 24 lights?

222
00:18:00,400 --> 00:18:01,100
$12.

223
00:18:02,320 --> 00:18:04,980
Equal to a week's wages for the average person.

224
00:18:05,560 --> 00:18:12,760
In 1916, tungsten replaced the carbon filament, making the lights brighter and longer lasting.

225
00:18:13,980 --> 00:18:19,420
In the 1920s, mechanization of light bulb production made Christmas lights more affordable.

226
00:18:22,140 --> 00:18:28,400
Companies experimented with bulb sizes, shapes, and textures in an attempt to give the customer variety.

227
00:18:30,680 --> 00:18:34,620
And in 1946, came a real Christmas sensation.

228
00:18:35,560 --> 00:18:38,940
That was the introduction of the Noma bubble light, which looked just like this one.

229
00:18:39,220 --> 00:18:44,500
It was the only light that moved and had a fantasy effect, and everybody just loved them.

230
00:18:45,440 --> 00:18:52,420
A bubble light consists of a liquid-filled tube, held above a small incandescent bulb by a plastic cup and cap.

231
00:18:54,320 --> 00:19:03,140
The liquid is methylene chloride, a chemical compound with a low boiling point, often used as a solvent in a wide variety of industrial applications.

232
00:19:05,560 --> 00:19:12,560
Inside the tube, a small mass of sugar or salt helps hold a thin amount of methylene chloride close to the lamp.

233
00:19:12,561 --> 00:19:19,560
The modest heat generated by the lamp is enough to cause the liquid to boil, and then recirculate.

234
00:19:19,561 --> 00:19:23,560
The mass helps spread the bubbles.

235
00:19:23,561 --> 00:19:30,560
Like the holiday light sets of Christmas past, bubble lights came attached to sockets and wiring.

236
00:19:30,561 --> 00:19:33,860
attached to sockets and wiring.

237
00:19:33,861 --> 00:19:36,700
While they were easy to hang, series-wired light sets

238
00:19:36,701 --> 00:19:41,280
had one infuriating fatal flaw.

239
00:19:41,281 --> 00:19:45,820
If just one bulb failed, the whole strand went dark

240
00:19:45,821 --> 00:19:49,940
because the electric circuit was broken.

241
00:19:49,941 --> 00:19:54,080
And it was no fun searching for the culprit.

242
00:19:54,081 --> 00:19:57,620
The solution to this big problem is a little mechanism,

243
00:19:57,621 --> 00:20:00,120
The shunt.

244
00:20:00,121 --> 00:20:05,960
Each bulb in a series has a coated or anodized aluminum wire wrapped around the base of the

245
00:20:05,961 --> 00:20:10,180
posts that support the filament.

246
00:20:10,181 --> 00:20:15,120
If the filament burns out, the coating on the wire burns off, allowing this wire to

247
00:20:15,121 --> 00:20:18,120
act as a shunt.

248
00:20:18,121 --> 00:20:21,980
This completes the circuit and keeps the set alight.

249
00:20:21,981 --> 00:20:26,780
remove the bulb and you've removed the shunt, causing the entire string to fail.

250
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:33,240
For consumers, the shunt means one bad bulb won't spoil the whole string.

251
00:20:36,680 --> 00:20:41,920
The latest in Christmas lighting tech is the LED, or light emitting diode.

252
00:20:42,700 --> 00:20:48,280
An LED is simply a small wafer of silicon that when electricity is run through it, illuminates.

253
00:20:48,281 --> 00:20:54,860
It uses a lot less power than an incandescent light bulb because it requires very little energy to illuminate a light-emitting diode.

254
00:20:55,220 --> 00:20:57,100
They can last extremely long time.

255
00:20:57,240 --> 00:21:00,180
Right now, the bulbs we're seeing last up to 50,000 hours.

256
00:21:00,660 --> 00:21:08,120
We're starting to see light-emitting diodes come out of our factories in the Far East that are spec'd at 200,000 hours of average life.

257
00:21:09,960 --> 00:21:14,900
Unlike incandescent lights that glow white and require colored bulbs or envelopes,

258
00:21:14,901 --> 00:21:21,900
LEDs can actually emit lights of varying colors.

259
00:21:21,901 --> 00:21:24,900
A light-emitting diode has two semiconductor sides.

260
00:21:24,901 --> 00:21:27,900
One side contains extra electrons,

261
00:21:27,901 --> 00:21:33,900
and the other contains holes to catch the electrons.

262
00:21:33,901 --> 00:21:37,900
When voltage is applied, it causes the electrons to fill the holes,

263
00:21:37,901 --> 00:21:41,900
so the current can flow through the diode.

264
00:21:41,901 --> 00:21:46,020
As an electron falls into a hole, a photon is released in the process.

265
00:21:47,300 --> 00:21:50,960
The distance of the electron's fall determines the color of the photon.

266
00:21:51,960 --> 00:21:58,380
So by engineering an LED with holes of a specific depth, a single specified color can be emitted.

267
00:22:03,700 --> 00:22:06,300
